                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 913,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 876,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Gives how much two vectors point the same way, with their sizes taken\nout: the cosine of the angle between them.\n\n1 means the same direction, 0 at right angles, -1 opposite. Size does\nnot matter: [3 4] and [6 8] point the same way and score 1. So two poses\nthat are the same shape at different scales, or two movements that are\nthe same gesture at different strengths, still read as alike. Reach for\nit to compare directions, poses or feature vectors when magnitude would\nget in the way.\n\nIt is the dot product of the two vectors divided by both their lengths\n(np.dot of two unit vectors gives the same number).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.cosine_similarity\n(no arguments)\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput 1:\nThe first vector (a list or a NumPy array is turned into a float32\ntensor). Receiving it calculates and sends.\n\ninput 2:\nThe second vector, the same length as the first. It must arrive\nAFRESH before EVERY input 1: the node does not reuse an old input 2, so\nsending input 1 on its own a second time sends nothing. In the demo a\nmetro re-sends both, input 2 first.\n\nBoth should be 1-D vectors. Two tables are compared column by column,\nwhich gives more than one number: an error is printed and nothing is\nsent. A vector of all zeros has no direction and gives 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe cosine, a plain float from -1 to 1.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nVector A is two sliders packed into [x y], starting at [3 4]. Vector B\nis the angle on the slider turned into a direction (make_direction\ngives [cos, sin]). The metro re-sends B and then A ten times a second.\nWith B at 53 degrees it points almost exactly along [3 4] and the\nanswer is near 1. Turn B to 143 degrees (right angles): 0. To 233:\n-1. Now make A shorter in the same direction - [1.5 2] - and nothing\nchanges: only the direction counts.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.dot is the same calculation without dividing by the lengths.\nt.normalize makes vectors length 1, after which the dot product is the\ncosine. t.dist and t.length compare positions and sizes instead.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 860,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Gives how much two vectors point the same way, with their sizes taken\nout: the cosine of the angle between them.\n\n1 means the same direction, 0 at right angles, -1 opposite. Size does\nnot matter: [3 4] and [6 8] point the same way and score 1. So two poses\nthat are the same shape at different scales, or two movements that are\nthe same gesture at different strengths, still read as alike. Reach for\nit to compare directions, poses or feature vectors when magnitude would\nget in the way.\n\nIt is the dot product of the two vectors divided by both their lengths\n(np.dot of two unit vectors gives the same number).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.cosine_similarity\n(no arguments)\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput 1:\nThe first vector (a list or a NumPy array is turned into a float32\ntensor). Receiving it calculates and sends.\n\ninput 2:\nThe second vector, the same length as the first. It must arrive\nAFRESH before EVERY input 1: the node does not reuse an old input 2, so\nsending input 1 on its own a second time sends nothing. In the demo a\nmetro re-sends both, input 2 first.\n\nBoth should be 1-D vectors. Two tables are compared column by column,\nwhich gives more than one number: an error is printed and nothing is\nsent. A vector of all zeros has no direction and gives 0.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe cosine, a plain float from -1 to 1.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nVector A is two sliders packed into [x y], starting at [3 4]. Vector B\nis the angle on the slider turned into a direction (make_direction\ngives [cos, sin]). The metro re-sends B and then A ten times a second.\nWith B at 53 degrees it points almost exactly along [3 4] and the\nanswer is near 1. Turn B to 143 degrees (right angles): 0. To 233:\n-1. Now make A shorter in the same direction - [1.5 2] - and nothing\nchanges: only the direction counts.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.dot is the same calculation without dividing by the lengths.\nt.normalize makes vectors length 1, after which the dot product is the\ncosine. t.dist and t.length compare positions and sizes instead."
                },
